<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Wehali</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1906 CE (approx.) · capital Laran</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The ritual centre of central Timor, whose ruler was honoured as the source of authority by many Tetun and Bunak domains.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Wewiku-Wehali lay on the south coast of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a>, in today's <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/malaka">Malaka</a> Regency. Its traditions place it at the origin of the land, the first dry ground to emerge from the sea. The register's start of 1500 reflects its appearance in Portuguese records of the 16th century, when sandalwood traders reached Timor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Wehali was ruled by a sacred figure who remained still and inactive, while executive rulers called liurai governed and dealt with outsiders. Many domains across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a> acknowledged Wehali as their ritual elder. Society was matrilineal. Sandalwood and beeswax were traded to Portuguese, Chinese and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gowa-sultanate">Makassar</a> merchants.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Portuguese attacked Wehali in 1642, weakening its influence. Divided between Portuguese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> spheres, its domains were brought under direct Dutch control by 1906. Wehali remains an important ritual centre in Indonesian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-timor">West Timor</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/wehali.jpg" alt="Wehali" width="320" height="267"><figcaption>Image: Nicolaes Visscher II · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolaes_Visscher_Indiae_Orientalis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1906 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Laran</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous Timorese religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tetun</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Indonesia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wewiku-Wehali, Waiwiku-Wehali</dd></dl></aside></div>

